## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical rate limiting, tenant isolation, and testability in an LLM gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant quotas and burst caps
- Token bucket and optional leaky bucket hybrids
- Isolation in Redis or in-memory stores

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerSec) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.refillPerSec = refillPerSec;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillPerSec);
    this.lastRefill = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor per-tenant leakage and detect abuse?
- How would you scale buckets across multiple gateway instances?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world needs balance cost, latency, and reliability across tenants. This asks for a concrete policy language, a routing strategy, and test plans under varying traffic. It also probes drift handling and failover.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant QoS policies
- Multi-model routing with cost/latency prediction
- Data locality and failover strategies
- Observability: audit logs, drift tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing sketch
function route(req, tenant) {
  const p = policy[tenant];
  const candidates = models.filter(m => p.preferredModels.includes(m.name));
  // pick cheapest that meets latency
  return candidates
    .map(m => ({m, est: predict(m, req)}))
    .filter(x => x.est.lat <= p.max_latency)
    .sort((a,b) => a.est.cost - b.est.cost)[0]?.m;
}
```

